# The tracker's one setting: where the firewall server is.
#
# Copy to .env.local for local work, or set it in the Vercel project's
# environment variables for a deployment. It is read at BUILD time -- a static
# site has nothing to ask at runtime -- so changing it means redeploying.
#
# Leave it empty and the tracker fetches its own origin, which is correct in
# both of the cases where the page and the server are the same thing: the
# `firewall` CLI serving /tracker, and `npm run dev`, whose proxy sends /api to
# localhost:842 (set FIREWALL_ORIGIN to move that).
#
# Set it to the origin of the machine running `firewall` when the page is
# hosted apart from it -- the public HTTPS end of whatever tunnel fronts it,
# scheme and host, no trailing path. That origin also has to name this site in
# its own FIREWALL_ALLOW_ORIGINS, or the browser blocks every read.
VITE_API_BASE=

# How far back the tracker keeps calls, in hours. Empty means as far back as the
# server will go, which is the right answer for a screen on the same network as
# the incident log.
#
# The hosted build sets this to 24 in vercel.json, so a deployment does not have
# to be told: a copy of the tracker on the public internet showing a day is a
# different proposition to one in the building showing the archive. It is one
# number doing three jobs -- what /api/log is asked for, what the page keeps of
# the answer, and which window chips are offered -- so a day here really is a
# day, and the "7 days" chip is gone rather than empty.
VITE_RETAIN_HOURS=
